S3 = {
"title": "Stay awake",
"line": "Jesus's disciples admire the Temple's great stones. He tells them every stone will fall, and that the Son of Man will come. Then he tells them not to calculate, but to watch.",
"plain": "A few days before his death, Jesus's disciples admired the huge stones of the Temple in Jerusalem. He told them it would be torn down, and then gave his longest speech about the future: wars, persecution, false messiahs, the sun darkened, and 'the Son of Man coming in clouds'. Forty years later the Romans destroyed the Temple. But is the speech about that, about the end of the world, or both? And what did Jesus want his followers to do about it? His answer is one word, repeated four times: watch.",
"why": "The last session taught us how apocalyptic writing works: images drawn from the Old Testament, cosmic language for historical events, and hope for people under pressure. Now we can read the one long apocalyptic speech of Jesus himself, in Mark 13, and see what he said about the end and what he refused to say.",
"answers": [
 "Did Jesus predict the destruction of the Temple?",
 "What is the Second Coming?",
 "Is Mark 13 about AD 70, the end of the world, or both?",
 "Did Jesus tell us when the end would come?"
],
"finding": "Jesus warned of the Temple's fall and of a coming of the Son of Man, but he refused to give a date: he told his followers to watch, not to calculate.",
"background": [
 {"h": "The story so far", "paras": [
  "In the first session the prophets promised a new creation and Daniel promised the resurrection. In the second we learned to read apocalypse: a curtain pulled back, in pictures taken from the Old Testament, to give courage to people under pressure.",
  "Now we come to Jesus. His message began with the words 'The time is fulfilled, and God's Kingdom is at hand!' (Mark 1:15). The 'Kingdom of God' meant God's rule finally coming on earth, as the prophets had promised. In Mark 13, a few days before his death, he speaks about how that will come."
 ], "key": "Jesus announced that God's kingdom was at hand; in Mark 13 he speaks of how it will come."},
 {"h": "Where we are in the story", "paras": [
  "It is the last week of Jesus's life, in Jerusalem, at Passover, around AD 30. If you have taken 'The last week', you followed his entry into the city and his action in the Temple courts, overturning the tables of the money-changers (Mark 11). Mark 12 is a series of arguments with the Temple's leaders.",
  "The Temple was one of the wonders of the ancient world. King Herod the Great had begun rebuilding it in about 20 BC, on a vast platform; some of its stones weighed hundreds of tonnes, and parts of the platform's western wall still stand. It was the centre of Jewish life, the place where heaven and earth met. To say it would be destroyed was close to blasphemy. At Jesus's trial witnesses claimed he had said he would destroy it (Mark 14:58)."
 ], "key": "Around AD 30, in his last week, Jesus has confronted the Temple; Herod's Temple was huge, and to foretell its fall was shocking."},
 {"h": "What happened in AD 70", "paras": [
  "In AD 66 the Jews of Judea revolted against Rome. After four years of war, the Roman general Titus besieged Jerusalem, and in August AD 70 the Temple was burned and the city destroyed. The Jewish historian Josephus, who watched the siege from the Roman side, describes famine, mass crucifixions and the Temple in flames; if you have taken 'One root, two branches', you read his account there. The Arch of Titus in Rome, still standing, shows Roman soldiers carrying off the great lampstand from the Temple.",
  "The early Christian historian Eusebius, writing around 325, says the Christians of Jerusalem left the city before the war, after a warning, and settled in Pella across the Jordan (Church History III.5.3). Historians debate how reliable that report is."
 ], "timeline": [
  {"year": "c. 20 BC", "text": "Herod the Great begins rebuilding the Temple"},
  {"year": "c. AD 30", "text": "Jesus's last week in Jerusalem; Mark 13"},
  {"year": "c. AD 50", "text": "Paul's first letter to the Thessalonians"},
  {"year": "AD 66", "text": "The Jewish revolt against Rome begins"},
  {"year": "c. AD 65–73", "text": "Most scholars date Mark's Gospel to these years"},
  {"year": "AD 70", "text": "Titus destroys Jerusalem and the Temple"},
  {"year": "AD 81", "text": "The Arch of Titus is set up in Rome"}
 ], "key": "In AD 70, after a four-year revolt, Rome destroyed Jerusalem and its Temple; Mark was written around those years."},
 {"h": "Why we read these passages", "paras": [
  "The main reading is the whole of Mark 13, Jesus's speech on the Mount of Olives, often called 'the little apocalypse'. We read it in three parts: the warnings, the 'abomination' and the coming of the Son of Man, and the call to watch.",
  "The comparison is the parable of the ten bridesmaids in Matthew 25, which Matthew places just after his version of the same speech. It shows what 'watch' means in a story, and it is the parable behind Bach's cantata in this session's music."
 ], "key": "Mark 13 in three parts, then Matthew 25's parable of the bridesmaids, which shows what watching means."}
],
"before": [
 "What two things do the disciples ask in verse 4? Does Jesus answer both?",
 "Find every time Jesus says 'watch' or 'be careful'. What does he seem most worried about?",
 "Does Jesus say when the end will come? Compare verses 30 and 32."
],
"readWhy": "Jesus's longest speech about the future, read in three parts: warnings, the coming of the Son of Man, and the call to watch.",
"read": [
 {"bible": "Mark 13:1-13", "title": "Not one stone on another",
  "context": "Jerusalem, around AD 30, a few days before Jesus's death. He leaves the Temple and crosses the Kidron valley to the Mount of Olives, which looks straight across at the Temple. Four of his first disciples ask him privately what he meant.",
  "commentary": [
   {"on": "Teacher, see what kind of stones and what kind of buildings!", "text": "The disciples are country men from Galilee, and the Temple was built to impress. Some of its stones were over ten metres long."},
   {"on": "There will not be left here one stone on another", "text": "A plain prediction of total destruction. Forty years later it happened. Sceptical scholars argue that Mark wrote after AD 70 and put the prophecy into Jesus's mouth; others point out that prophets had foretold the Temple's fall before (Micah 3:12; Jeremiah 26), and that a Jewish prophet called Jesus son of Ananias did the same in the 60s, according to Josephus (Jewish War VI.5.3). It is not hard to believe Jesus said it."},
   {"on": "Tell us, when will these things be? What is the sign", "text": "Two questions: when, and what sign? The rest of the speech is Jesus's answer, and it is not quite the answer they wanted."},
   {"on": "Be careful that no one leads you astray.", "text": "The first words of the answer are not a timetable but a warning. The greatest danger is not the catastrophe but being deceived by those who claim to know."},
   {"on": "When you hear of wars and rumors of wars, don't be troubled. For those must happen, but the end is not yet.", "text": "This verse alone should have stopped many confident predictions. Wars and earthquakes are not signs that the end is here. They are 'the beginning of birth pains': the world groaning, as it always has, towards something new."},
   {"on": "The Good News must first be preached to all the nations.", "text": "Before the end, the message goes to every nation. History has a task to complete. This one verse turns the waiting into mission."},
   {"on": "he who endures to the end will be saved", "text": "Not escaping suffering but enduring it. Jesus promises persecution, arrests and betrayal within families, and asks for faithfulness."}
  ]},
 {"bible": "Mark 13:14-27", "title": "The abomination, and the coming of the Son of Man",
  "context": "Jesus now moves to the crisis itself, using phrases from Daniel and the prophets that his hearers would have recognised.",
  "commentary": [
   {"on": "when you see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet", "text": "In Daniel (9:27; 11:31; 12:11) the 'abomination' was the pagan altar Antiochus IV set up in the Temple in 167 BC, an object so horrifying that it empties the holy place. Jesus warns of something like it again. Mark's aside, 'let the reader understand', suggests that his first readers could see it coming."},
   {"on": "then let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains", "text": "Very practical, local advice: Judea, housetops, fields, winter. This is about a real war in a real country, not the end of the universe. It fits the Roman siege of Jerusalem."},
   {"on": "Look, here is the Christ!", "text": "Josephus describes many would-be deliverers in the years before AD 70. Jesus's followers are not to be taken in by anyone who claims to be him."},
   {"on": "the sun will be darkened, the moon will not give its light", "text": "This is the language of the prophets for the fall of empires: Isaiah used it of Babylon (13:10) and of Edom (34:4). Cosmic language for an earth-shaking event in history, as the last session taught us. It may also point beyond history, to the end."},
   {"on": "Then they will see the Son of Man coming in clouds with great power and glory.", "text": "Daniel 7:13 again: 'one like a son of man' coming 'with the clouds of the sky'. In Daniel he comes to God, to receive the kingdom. Christians have mostly read this verse as Jesus's return to earth, the Second Coming. Some scholars, such as N. T. Wright, read it as his vindication: the fall of the Temple would show that God had given him the kingdom. The verse is at the centre of the debate."},
   {"on": "he will send out his angels, and will gather together his chosen ones from the four winds", "text": "The end is a gathering, not a scattering. The prophets promised that God would gather his scattered people (Isaiah 43:5–6). Here the gathering is from the whole earth."}
  ]},
 {"bible": "Mark 13:28-37", "title": "Watch",
  "context": "Jesus ends with two short parables, a saying about timing and a command.",
  "commentary": [
   {"on": "When the branch has now become tender, and produces its leaves, you know that the summer is near", "text": "The fig tree in Israel loses its leaves in winter; when they come back, summer is close. Signs can be read, but only roughly: you know the season, not the day."},
   {"on": "this generation will not pass away until all these things happen", "text": "The hardest verse in the chapter. 'This generation' usually means the people then alive. If 'all these things' includes the coming of the Son of Man and the end of the world, Jesus seems to have been wrong. If it means the fall of the Temple, it came true within forty years. The fifth session of this journey, still to come, faces this objection at full strength."},
   {"on": "Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away.", "text": "An astonishing claim. In Isaiah 40:8 it is 'the word of our God' that 'stands forever'. Jesus says it of his own words."},
   {"on": "But of that day or that hour no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father.", "text": "Here Jesus refuses to set a date and says that he himself does not know it. Every person in history who has set a date for the end has had to ignore this verse. It also puzzled the Church Fathers: how could the Son not know? Many answered that he did not know it in his human nature."},
   {"on": "Watch, keep alert, and pray; for you don't know when the time is.", "text": "This is the point of the whole speech. The disciples asked 'when?'; Jesus answers 'watch'. Not calculation but readiness."},
   {"on": "given authority to his servants, and to each one his work", "text": "Watching is not staring at the sky. The servants have work to do while the master is away. Waiting for the end, in Jesus's picture, means being faithful in your work now."},
   {"on": "What I tell you, I tell all: Watch.", "text": "The last word of Jesus's teaching in Mark before the passion begins. It is said to four disciples on a hillside and, Mark insists, to all his readers."}
  ]}
],
"compare": {"title": "The wise and foolish bridesmaids", "why": "Matthew sets this parable just after his version of the same speech. It shows what 'watch' means in a story: not knowing the hour, but being ready for it.",
 "parts": [
  {"bible": "Matthew 25:1-13", "title": "The bridegroom is coming",
   "context": "In a village wedding of Jesus's time, the bridegroom came at night to the bride's home and led a procession, with lamps, to his own family's house for the feast. Young women of the village went out to meet him. The 'virgins' are bridesmaids.",
   "commentary": [
    {"on": "Five of them were foolish, and five were wise.", "text": "The two groups look the same from outside. All have lamps, all are waiting, all fall asleep. The difference only shows when the moment comes."},
    {"on": "while the bridegroom delayed, they all slumbered and slept", "text": "The parable expects a delay. Matthew was writing when the first generation of Christians was dying; the parable tells them not to be surprised that the bridegroom is late."},
    {"on": "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out.", "text": "Readiness cannot be borrowed at the last minute. Many interpreters have taken the oil as the good works or the faith that only a life can build up."},
    {"on": "those who were ready went in with him to the wedding feast, and the door was shut", "text": "The end in this story is a wedding feast, the prophets' picture of joy (Isaiah 25:6). Being shut out is the tragedy. The parable is a warning, but what is being missed is a party."},
    {"on": "Watch therefore, for you don't know the day nor the hour", "text": "The same word as Mark 13: watch. The parable makes it concrete: keep your lamp filled."}
   ]}
 ]},
"lecture": {"sections": [
 {"h": "What the Second Coming means", "paras": [
  "The New Testament's most common word for Christ's return is parousia, Greek for 'presence' or 'arrival'. It was used for the visit of an emperor or governor to a city, when citizens went out to meet him. Christians took it for the coming of Christ in glory, which they also called 'the day of the Lord'.",
  "All the historic creeds teach it. The Apostles' Creed says 'from thence he shall come to judge the quick and the dead', 'quick' meaning living. The Nicene Creed adds 'with glory' and 'whose kingdom shall have no end'. Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant churches all affirm it. What they mean by it, and how much detail they claim to know, varies a great deal.",
  "At its simplest, the Second Coming means this: the story will not just stop or fade away. The one who came in weakness, and was crucified, will be seen as he really is, and everything will be put right in his presence."
 ], "key": "The parousia, Christ's coming in glory, is in all the creeds; at its simplest it means the story ends with Christ seen as he is and all put right."},
 {"h": "Three ways to read Mark 13", "table": {"cols": ["Reading", "What the speech is about", "Strength", "Difficulty"], "rows": [
  ["The end of the world", "Mostly the last days and Christ's return", "The cosmic language; the coming of the Son of Man", "The local details: Judea, housetops, winter; and 'this generation'"],
  ["AD 70", "Mostly the fall of Jerusalem; the 'coming' is Jesus's vindication", "The disciples' question is about the Temple; it came true within a generation", "Seems to empty the Second Coming from a key text"],
  ["Both", "The fall of the Temple as a near event and a picture of the final end", "Prophets often saw a near crisis and the final day together", "Hard to tell where one ends and the other begins"]
 ], "note": "Most readers, ancient and modern, take some form of the third view."}, "paras": [
  "Mark 13 is one of the most argued-over chapters in the Bible. There are three main readings."
 ], "key": "Mark 13 has been read as about the end of the world, about AD 70, or both; most take some form of 'both'."},
 {"h": "Prophetic foreshortening", "paras": [
  "Why might a prophet speak of two events as one? A common illustration is a range of mountains seen from far away: two peaks look side by side, though miles of valley lie between them. Old Testament prophets often spoke of a coming disaster, such as the fall of Babylon, in the language of the end of all things.",
  "Read that way, Mark 13 answers the disciples' question about the Temple, and uses its fall as a window on the end. The near event shows what the final one will be like: sudden, judging, and a gathering of God's people. Many Church Fathers read it so, and so do many modern scholars.",
  "Sceptics reply that this is a convenient way to save a failed prediction. That objection is real, and it deserves its own session."
 ], "key": "Prophets often saw a near crisis and the final end together; read so, the Temple's fall is a window on the end, though sceptics call this convenient."},
 {"h": "Not even the Son", "paras": [
  "Mark 13:32 is remarkable. It is not the kind of saying the early Church would invent: it says Jesus did not know something. Matthew keeps it (24:36); Luke leaves it out. Arius, in the fourth century, used it to argue that the Son was less than the Father. Athanasius replied that Christ, being truly human, did not know it in his human mind, though he knew it as God (Against the Arians III.42–46).",
  "Whatever we make of that, the saying cuts off every attempt to set a date. William Miller, an American Baptist preacher, worked out from Daniel that Christ would return in 1843, then on 22 October 1844. Thousands sold their farms; when nothing happened, it became known as the Great Disappointment. Many others have set dates since, and every one has passed.",
  "Jesus's own teaching was the opposite: the end is certain, its date is unknown, and so the right response is not calculation but faithfulness."
 ], "key": "Jesus said not even the Son knew the day; every date-setter, like William Miller in 1844, has had to ignore it."},
 {"h": "What watching means", "paras": [
  "'Watch' in Mark 13 is the opposite of sleep, not the opposite of work. The servants in the last parable each have their work. Paul tells the Thessalonians, who had stopped working because they thought the end was near, that anyone who will not work should not eat (2 Thessalonians 3:10).",
  "So the Church has understood watching as a way of living. The monks kept vigils at night, praying the psalms before dawn. Advent, the four weeks before Christmas, begins each year with the readings of Mark 13 and the parables of watching: the Church year starts by looking to the end. In 1780, when a sudden darkness fell over New England and members of the Connecticut legislature thought the Day of Judgement had come and wanted to adjourn, Abraham Davenport is said to have replied that if it was, he wished to be found doing his duty, and asked for candles.",
  "That is what Jesus asked: not to know the hour, but to be found doing your work."
 ], "key": "Watching means faithful work and prayer, not sky-gazing: Advent begins with Mark 13, and Davenport asked for candles to keep working."},
 {"h": "Prophecy and the date of the Gospels", "paras": [
  "Mark 13 matters to an argument about when the Gospels were written. If Jesus could not have foretold the Temple's fall, then any Gospel that contains the prediction must have been written after AD 70. Many scholars date Mark to around 70 for this and other reasons.",
  "Others reply that the reasoning assumes what it sets out to prove, and point to a detail. Mark's version is vague: 'the abomination of desolation', a phrase from Daniel, and flight to the mountains. Luke's version, which most scholars date after 70, is far more specific: 'when you see Jerusalem surrounded by armies' (Luke 21:20), and 'they will fall by the edge of the sword, and will be led captive into all the nations' (21:24). If Mark had known how the siege went, they ask, why did he not say so? And Mark's warning to pray that the flight would not be in winter fits poorly with what happened: the Temple fell in August.",
  "The evidence does not settle the date. But it does show that Mark 13 reads more like a warning given before the event than a description written after it."
 ], "key": "Some date Mark after AD 70 because of the prediction; others note Mark is vaguer than Luke 21, which suggests a warning given before the event."},
 {"h": "False messiahs", "paras": [
  "Jesus's warning against people who say 'I am he' was not abstract. Josephus describes several leaders in the decades after Jesus who gathered followers with promises of deliverance. A man called Theudas led a crowd to the Jordan around AD 45, promising to part the river; the Roman governor's cavalry killed him (Antiquities 20.5.1; compare Acts 5:36). A man known as 'the Egyptian' led a crowd to the Mount of Olives around AD 55, promising that the walls of Jerusalem would fall at his command; the governor Felix attacked them (Antiquities 20.8.6; Jewish War II.13.5; compare Acts 21:38). During the siege itself, Josephus says, a false prophet led people into the Temple courts to await signs of deliverance, and they died in the flames (Jewish War VI.5.2).",
  "Against all of them, Jesus's advice is the same: do not believe it, and do not run after them. It is the advice that every later generation, offered a leader who claims to know when the end will come, has needed to hear."
 ], "key": "Josephus records would-be deliverers, such as Theudas and 'the Egyptian', before AD 70; Jesus's warning was concrete, and remains needed."}

], "refs": [
 "Mark 1:15; 11–14; 13:1–37; Matthew 24:36; 25:1–13; Daniel 7:13; 9:27; 11:31; 12:11; Isaiah 13:10; 34:4; 40:8; 43:5–6; Micah 3:12; Jeremiah 26; 2 Thessalonians 3:10.",
 "Josephus, Jewish War VI.5.3 (Jesus son of Ananias); VI.4–5 (the burning of the Temple). Eusebius, Church History III.5.3 (the flight to Pella). Athanasius, Against the Arians III.42–46.",
 "N. T. Wright, Jesus and the Victory of God (1996), chapter 8; R. T. France, The Gospel of Mark (NIGTC, 2002); Adela Yarbro Collins, Mark (Hermeneia, 2007); Dale C. Allison, Constructing Jesus (2010).",
 "On William Miller: David L. Rowe, God's Strange Work: William Miller and the End of the World (2008). On Davenport and the Dark Day of 19 May 1780: Timothy Dwight's account, retold in John Greenleaf Whittier's poem 'Abraham Davenport'."
]},
"terms": ["Parousia", "Kingdom of God", "Son of Man", "Abomination of desolation", "Day of the Lord", "Advent", "Temple"],
"debate": {"question": "Is Mark 13 about AD 70 or the end of the world?", "positions": [
 {"name": "Mainly AD 70", "who": "N. T. Wright; R. T. France (on 13:24–31); many preterist readers", "view": "The disciples asked about the Temple, and Jesus answered. The cosmic language is the prophets' way of describing the fall of a power, and the 'coming of the Son of Man' is Daniel's picture of his vindication. 'This generation' then means exactly what it says. The real end is spoken of only from verse 32.", "evidence": "The local details (Judea, housetops, winter); Isaiah 13:10 used of Babylon; Daniel 7:13 where the Son of Man comes to God; the Temple fell within forty years."},
 {"name": "Mainly the end", "who": "Many traditional readers; many modern scholars, such as Dale Allison and Adela Yarbro Collins", "view": "The speech moves from the war to the final coming of the Son of Man to gather the elect from the ends of the earth. That did not happen in AD 70. Jesus, like other Jewish prophets of his day, expected the end soon after the Temple's fall.", "evidence": "Angels gathering the elect from the whole earth (13:27); Paul and the earliest Christians expected Christ's return from heaven (1 Thessalonians 4:16); Matthew joins the Temple's fall and 'the end of the age' (24:3)."},
 {"name": "Both, seen together", "who": "Many Church Fathers; many Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant commentators", "view": "Like the prophets, Jesus saw the near judgement on Jerusalem and the final judgement together. The first came true within a generation; the second is still awaited, and the first is its pledge.", "evidence": "The prophets' foreshortening; the shift at verse 32 from 'these things' to 'that day'; the command to watch, which covers both."}
], "agree": "All agree that Jesus foretold the fall of the Temple, refused to give a date for the end, and commanded his followers to watch."},
"look": {"art": None, "why": "No painting for this session. The National Gallery of Art's open collection has no painting of the wise and foolish bridesmaids, of the fall of the Temple or of the coming of the Son of Man that is free to use here, and we do not use a stand-in.", "points": []},
"music": {"id": "wachet-auf", "why": "'Sleepers, wake!': Bach's cantata of 1731 on the parable of the wise and foolish bridesmaids, built on Philipp Nicolai's hymn of 1599. Listen for the watchmen's call and the bridegroom's arrival."},
"think": {"library": "who-was-jesus", "depth": 2, "why": "What Christians believe about who Jesus is, including the one who will come again."},
"learn": {"memory": "mark-13-33", "why": "The point of Jesus's whole speech about the end, in one line."},
"check": [
 {"q": "The disciples ask 'when?' What is the main point of Jesus's answer?", "options": ["He gives them a precise timetable of signs", "He warns them not to be deceived, refuses to give the date, and tells them to watch", "He says the end will never come", "He tells them to flee Jerusalem at once"], "answer": 1, "why": "'No one knows... Watch': the answer to 'when?' is readiness, not calculation."},
 {"q": "Why do many scholars think verses 14–20 are about a real war in Judea?", "options": ["Because they mention Rome by name", "Because of the local, practical details: Judea, the mountains, housetops, fields and winter", "Because they quote Josephus", "Because they are in poetry"], "answer": 1, "why": "Advice to flee to the mountains before winter fits the siege of Jerusalem, not the end of the universe."},
 {"q": "Why does 'the sun will be darkened' not have to mean a literal astronomical event?", "options": ["Because eclipses did not happen in the ancient world", "Because the prophets used such language for the fall of empires, as Isaiah did of Babylon", "Because Jesus said it was a parable", "Because the sun is mentioned only once in the Bible"], "answer": 1, "why": "Isaiah 13:10 uses it of Babylon's fall: cosmic language for earth-shaking events in history."},
 {"q": "In Daniel 7:13, where does 'one like a son of man' come 'with the clouds'?", "options": ["Down to earth", "To God, the Ancient of Days, to receive a kingdom", "To Jerusalem, to destroy the Temple", "To Babylon"], "answer": 1, "why": "That is why some scholars read Mark 13:26 as vindication rather than return; most Christians have read it as the Second Coming."},
 {"q": "What does Mark 13:32 ('not even the Son') rule out?", "options": ["Belief in the Second Coming", "Any attempt to calculate the date of the end", "Belief that Jesus was human", "The fall of the Temple"], "answer": 1, "why": "If the Son did not know the day, no one can calculate it; William Miller's 1844 prediction ignored this."},
 {"q": "According to the parable of the servants (13:34) and the bridesmaids, what does 'watching' involve?", "options": ["Leaving work to wait for the end", "Studying the signs to set a date", "Being faithful in the work you have been given, ready at any time", "Sleeping until the bridegroom comes"], "answer": 2, "why": "Each servant has his work; the wise have oil in their lamps. Watching is a way of living now."}
],
"tutorial": {"question": "Did Jesus predict the end of the world?", "approach": "Read Mark 13 carefully. Set out what Jesus clearly predicted (the fall of the Temple), what is disputed (the coming of the Son of Man and 'this generation'), and what he refused to predict (the day). Weigh the three readings, using Daniel and the prophets. End with what he asked his followers to do.",
 "strong": [
  "Distinguishes the two questions in 13:4 and the fall of the Temple from the final end.",
  "Uses the local details of 13:14–20 and AD 70 as evidence.",
  "Explains the background of Daniel 7:13 and prophetic cosmic language.",
  "Weighs the AD 70, end-of-the-world and 'both' readings fairly.",
  "Gives due weight to 13:32 and the command to watch.",
  "Recognises that 13:30 is a real difficulty."
 ]},
"reading": [
 {"author": "Tom Wright", "title": "Mark for Everyone", "year": 2001, "level": "start", "why": "A short, clear commentary for ordinary readers."},
 {"author": "R. T. France", "title": "The Gospel of Mark (NIGTC)", "year": 2002, "level": "deeper", "why": "A careful commentary that reads most of Mark 13 as about AD 70."},
 {"author": "Adela Yarbro Collins", "title": "Mark (Hermeneia)", "year": 2007, "level": "scholars", "why": "A major commentary that reads Mark 13 as apocalyptic prophecy of the end."}
],
"practice": "Live it this week: Jesus's servants each have their work while the master is away. Choose one task you have been putting off that someone is relying on you for, and do it this week as if he might come back today.",
"reflect": "If you knew the world would end next year, what would you stop doing, and what would you keep doing? What does your answer say about your life now?",
"takeaways": [
 "In Mark 13 Jesus foretold the fall of the Temple, which happened in AD 70, and the coming of the Son of Man in Daniel's words.",
 "The speech can be read as about AD 70, the end, or both; 'this generation' is its hardest verse.",
 "Jesus refused to give a date ('not even the Son' knows) and told his followers to watch: to be found faithful in their work."
]
}
